1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,033
Both leaves are Arabidopsis. Only one has
hairs.

2
00:00:07,033 --> 00:00:15,133
Follow one hair to find out why.

3
00:00:15,133 --> 00:00:24,100
This branched hair is a trichome.

4
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:33,100
The entire hair is one cell of the leaf
surface.

5
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:39,100
Researchers compared hairy and smooth plants.

6
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:46,067
They read the GL1 gene and tested its
function.

7
00:00:46,067 --> 00:00:51,133
The vacuole fills much of this leaf cell.

8
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:56,100
The nucleus lies beside the vacuole.

9
00:00:56,100 --> 00:01:04,067
The nucleus holds DNA. GL1 is part of that
DNA.

10
00:01:04,067 --> 00:01:11,067
DNA has four letters. Find the middle C in
TCT.

11
00:01:11,067 --> 00:01:18,067
These atoms form the same short piece of GL1
DNA.

12
00:01:18,067 --> 00:01:23,033
The reference plant has C at this position.

13
00:01:23,033 --> 00:01:28,033
The variant has T instead. TCT becomes TTT.

14
00:01:28,033 --> 00:01:37,033
The cell makes an RNA copy of this DNA
message.

15
00:01:37,033 --> 00:01:42,033
A ribosome reads RNA in threes called codons.

16
00:01:42,033 --> 00:01:48,067
These codons select amino acids, the building
blocks of proteins.

17
00:01:48,067 --> 00:01:57,033
UCU gives serine. UUU gives phenylalanine
instead.

18
00:01:57,033 --> 00:02:07,000
GL1 helps turn on the genes a leaf cell needs
to form a hair.

19
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:18,000
The altered GL1 protein activates these genes
poorly.

20
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,967
Predict the surface of the next leaf.

21
00:02:25,967 --> 00:02:30,000
Add working GL1 to test your prediction.

22
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,967
Compare the new leaf with the untreated
control.

23
00:02:33,967 --> 00:02:39,000
Only the new leaf with working GL1 grows
hairs.

24
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:50,000
One DNA letter changed the GL1 protein. The
early leaf grew without hairs.
